# Import boundaries, enforced in CI (make lint-imports / the CI backend job).
# A boundary that nothing checks is a boundary that is already broken.
#
# The load-bearing rule (docs/adr/0015): the engines layer owns computation and
# nothing else. It may import the shared kernel and stdlib — never a module, never
# FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, Pydantic or any I/O library. Modules may import engines
# freely, so this is a one-directional fence.
#
# `app.shared` is forbidden to both, which is the hole ADR-0022 records: it was on
# neither list, so a kernel or engine module could import `SystemClock` or
# `new_uuid` with CI fully green. Ambience is forbidden with it — `random`, `uuid`,
# `secrets` and `time` are the stdlib routes to the same thing, and ADR-0015's
# three purity rules (no clock, no randomness, no hidden state) are worth exactly
# what enforces them.
#
# Engines are also fenced from *each other* when two of them answer questions that
# could be confused. **Two ship today** — RacketSkill and Match Fit (docs/adr/0093) —
# and the pair below is the shape this comment used to describe in advance: two
# `forbidden` contracts and not one, because a single contract with overlapping
# source and forbidden lists would also forbid each package from importing itself.
# The question they could be confused about is a *level*: RacketSkill defines μ, σ,
# the bands and the σ-combination rule, and Match Fit reads them by calling
# `racketskill/core` through the module that composes them — never by re-deriving
# one. Two engines with two opinions about a level is the failure this fence exists
# for, and ADR-0093 names it by name.
#
# `datetime` is forbidden to the **engines only**. The kernel's time module is its
# one legitimate home; an engine receives an instant rather than constructing one.
# `allow_indirect_imports = true` is what makes that expressible: an engine importing
# the kernel, which imports `datetime`, is not an engine reading a clock. Module
# granularity is also why this file cannot be the whole fence — `datetime` is legal
# in the kernel while `datetime.now()` is not, and that half is an AST walk in
# tests/unit/test_kernel_purity.py.
#
# Configuration is fenced out of the layers where business rules live. **The
# environment may select configuration defaults; it may never select behaviour a
# domain test could observe** — the rule `app/core/config.py`'s own docstring opens
# with. A use case reading `settings.APP_ENV` to pick a deadline is a rule that is
# true on a laptop and false in production, and the domain test suite cannot see the
# difference. So the settings module is forbidden to the domain and application
# layers outright, rather than trusted to be used carefully.
#
# `pydantic_settings` and `os` are forbidden beside it for the same reason ambience
# is forbidden to the engines above: declaring a second `BaseSettings` or reading
# `os.environ` directly is the same rule broken by a different import. Neither
# appears anywhere in these four layers today, so this costs nothing to hold.
#
# The two `app.modules.*` arms are wildcards so that a context nobody has written yet
# — Clubs, Courts, Bookings, Matches — is fenced on the day it is created rather than
# on the day somebody remembers to come back here. That convenience carries a cost
# worth knowing, and it was measured rather than assumed: **a wildcard matching
# nothing is reported KEPT**, so an arm that stops matching becomes decoration in
# silence. It is why scripts/test-purity-gates.sh drives a probe through *each* of
# the four arms separately instead of through one and inferring the rest.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = true` agrees with the contracts above. The specific
# coupling this note used to cite — every module's `domain/errors.py` taking its error
# base classes from `app.core.exception_handlers` — **is gone**: #101 split the taxonomy
# into `app/shared/domain/errors.py`, and the contract below now forbids the chain
# outright. What remains is the general form of the same argument, and it is why the
# flag stays: a domain layer may legitimately import a module that is itself on the
# outside of the configuration fence, and strict transitive enforcement would then go
# red naming a domain module that had done nothing. The claim made here is the one that
# stays checkable whatever the graph does: no domain or application module *reads the
# settings module*.
#
# `sqlalchemy` is fenced beside the three web packages because `backend/CLAUDE.md`
# forbids all four to a domain layer in one breath, and enforcing three of the four
# would leave the rule looking checked when a quarter of it was not. It is green on
# today's tree — persistence lives in `infrastructure/`, which is on the outside of
# this fence — so it costs nothing to hold and catches the ORM model somebody will
# one day reach for while writing an aggregate.
#
# The imaging and object-store libraries are fenced the same way, and for the reason
# ADR-0091 exists: photo bytes live behind a `PhotoStorage` port and the re-encode
# behind an `ImageTranscoder` port, and *behind a port* is only true while nothing on
# the inside can reach the library. That fence sets `allow_indirect_imports = false`
# deliberately, because the interesting violation is the indirect one — a use case
# that constructs `PillowImageTranscoder` itself has bypassed the port exactly as
# completely as one that imports Pillow, and with the flag on it would read as KEPT.
# It is green on today's tree with the flag off, which is what makes it affordable.
#
# The framework contract below is the opposite case to the configuration one, and sets
# `allow_indirect_imports = false` deliberately. The violation it exists for was
# **only ever indirect** — no domain module has ever imported `fastapi` directly, and
# with the flag on this contract would have been green on the broken tree, which is
# the same as not existing. `scripts/test-purity-gates.sh` therefore drives an
# *indirect* probe through it as well as a direct one; the direct probe alone would
# still pass if somebody flipped the flag.

[importlinter]
root_package = app
include_external_packages = True

[importlinter:contract:engines-are-pure]
name = Engines import only the shared kernel — never a module, framework, I/O library or ambience
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.engines
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules
    app.api
    app.application
    app.infrastructure
    app.bootstrap
    app.core
    app.main
    app.shared
    fastapi
    starlette
    sqlalchemy
    pydantic
    pydantic_settings
    alembic
    asyncpg
    aiosqlite
    redis
    aiohttp
    httpx
    celery
    jwt
    bcrypt
    random
    uuid
    secrets
    time
    datetime
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:racketskill-does-not-know-match-fit]
name = The RacketSkill engine does not import the Match Fit engine
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.engines.racketskill
forbidden_modules =
    app.engines.matchfit
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:match-fit-does-not-know-racketskill]
name = The Match Fit engine does not import the RacketSkill engine
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.engines.matchfit
forbidden_modules =
    app.engines.racketskill
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:kernel-is-innermost]
name = The shared kernel depends on nothing in the app, no framework or I/O library, and no ambience
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.kernel
forbidden_modules =
    app.engines
    app.modules
    app.api
    app.application
    app.infrastructure
    app.bootstrap
    app.core
    app.main
    app.shared
    fastapi
    starlette
    sqlalchemy
    pydantic
    pydantic_settings
    alembic
    asyncpg
    aiosqlite
    redis
    aiohttp
    httpx
    celery
    jwt
    bcrypt
    random
    uuid
    secrets
    time
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:domain-does-not-know-the-framework-or-the-orm]
name = No domain layer depends on a framework or the ORM, directly or through what it imports
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.shared.domain
    app.modules.*.domain
forbidden_modules =
    fastapi
    starlette
    pydantic
    sqlalchemy
allow_indirect_imports = false

[importlinter:contract:configuration-cannot-reach-a-business-rule]
name = The domain and application layers cannot read configuration
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.application
    app.shared.domain
    app.modules.*.domain
    app.modules.*.application
forbidden_modules =
    app.core.config
    pydantic_settings
    os
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:a-business-layer-cannot-reach-an-imaging-or-object-store-library]
name = No domain or application layer imports an imaging, object-store or HTTP-client library
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.application
    app.shared.domain
    app.modules.*.domain
    app.modules.*.application
forbidden_modules =
    PIL
    pillow_heif
    aioboto3
    boto3
    botocore
    aiohttp
allow_indirect_imports = false

# The ephemeral store is fenced out of the business layers on exactly the argument
# ADR-0091 makes for photo bytes: *behind a port* is only true while nothing on the
# inside can reach the library. The two ledgers are ports in
# `matchmaking/domain/ledgers.py`, and what makes their fail-soft posture a promise
# rather than a hope is that every ledger leaves the composition root wrapped — an
# unwrapped adapter raises, and a raise on either path empties a surface to protect
# nobody. A use case reaching for Redis itself is a use case that has skipped that.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = false`, for the reason the imaging fence sets it: the
# interesting violation is the indirect one. `RedisExposureLedger` takes a `Redis`,
# so a use case importing the adapter to construct one is caught here as well as a
# use case importing the client — and with the flag on, the first would read as
# KEPT.
[importlinter:contract:a-business-layer-cannot-reach-the-ephemeral-store]
name = No domain or application layer imports Redis, or an adapter over it
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.application
    app.shared.domain
    app.modules.*.domain
    app.modules.*.application
forbidden_modules =
    redis
allow_indirect_imports = false

# The matcher ends at a ranked list and *asking somebody* begins in `challenges`,
# and the whole point of the split is that the matcher reads challenges through a
# port rather than owning them (#221, #250). The arrow therefore runs
# `matchmaking -> challenges` and never back — which is a rule about one import
# statement, and so exactly the kind that is broken by somebody in a hurry
# reaching for `SentFromSearch` or `Side` because a type with the right shape
# already exists one directory over. Both are deliberately spelled twice; this is
# what keeps the second spelling from being deleted as duplication.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = true`, for the reason the configuration fence sets it:
# `challenges` legitimately imports `player_profile` (the `Window` ADR-0100 puts
# there) and the Match Fit engine (`SearchMode`), and a strict contract would one
# day go red naming a module that had done nothing. The violation this exists for
# is the direct one.
[importlinter:contract:challenges-does-not-know-the-matcher]
name = The Challenges context does not import the matcher
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.challenges
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.matchmaking
allow_indirect_imports = true

# The same rule one context over, and it is the same rule rather than a copy of
# it: `games` is a **provider**, it signs `MatchHistoryQuery`'s missing half
# through a composing adapter that lives in the matcher's own infrastructure, and
# it does not know a matcher exists (docs/adr/0137). It has more second spellings
# than `challenges` does, not fewer — `PLAYED_COUNTERPART_BOUND` against
# `PAIRWISE_COUNTERPART_BOUND`, `PlayedOutcome` against `PairwiseOutcome`,
# `GameSide` against two other `Side`s — and each of them is a type with the
# right shape sitting one directory over, which is exactly what somebody in a
# hurry reaches for. `games_match_history.py` refuses to import when the two
# bounds disagree, and that guards the **value**; this guards the **direction**,
# which is the half a constant cannot check.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = true`, for the reason its neighbour sets it: `games`
# legitimately imports `player_profile` (the `Window` ADR-0100 puts there) and
# the Match Fit engine (`SearchMode`), and a strict contract would one day go red
# naming a module that had done nothing.
[importlinter:contract:games-does-not-know-the-matcher]
name = The Games context does not import the matcher
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.games
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.matchmaking
allow_indirect_imports = true

# The third of the same rule, and the one most likely to be broken, because
# `player_score` is the context whose whole output is a number the matcher reads
# (docs/adr/0140). It publishes `PublishedScoreQuery` in its own vocabulary and
# an adapter in the matcher's own infrastructure translates it into
# `PlayerScoreReading` — so the shapes on the two sides are near-identical and
# sitting one directory apart, which is the exact configuration somebody in a
# hurry collapses. Importing `RatioStrand` here instead of declaring
# `StrandRatio` would make this context depend on the consumer it exists to
# serve, and would put the matcher's rounding inside the number on a player's own
# profile.
#
# It reads two *other* contexts on purpose and that is not what this fences: the
# arrow runs `player_score -> games` and `player_score -> challenges`, the same
# direction the matcher's own composing adapters take, and neither provider knows
# this context exists.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = true`, for the reason its two neighbours set it: it
# legitimately imports the Match Fit engine for `ReliabilityStrand`, which is the
# closed vocabulary a second spelling here would fork.
[importlinter:contract:player-score-does-not-know-the-matcher]
name = The Player Score context does not import the matcher
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.player_score
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.matchmaking
allow_indirect_imports = true

# The fourth of the same rule, and then — uniquely so far — the same rule pointed
# back the other way. `sportsmanship` does not import the matcher, for the reason
# its three neighbours do not: it is a provider, and a provider that knew its
# consumer would eventually be shaped by it.
#
# The second contract is the one that is actually load-bearing, and it is a
# **product** decision rather than a layering one (docs/adr/0154). The matcher
# ignores sportsmanship entirely — not in the floor, not as a term, not as a
# tie-break — because it is the one strand a person can weaponise and there is no
# appeal route behind it. A reader seeing `3,1 / 5 · 12 players` and deciding for
# themselves is the product working; the app halving somebody's exposure on the
# same evidence, silently, with nothing they can answer, is not. That decision has
# no other enforcement: nothing goes red the day a Match Fit term starts reading
# a star average, because it would look like every other term beside it. This is
# what goes red.
#
# It is also why the reliability pill is the counted-only number: a star average
# is not a ratio out of a hundred and cannot be a strand in a weighted mean over
# ratios, and had the pill included opinions the matcher ignores, the number a
# player can see would not be the number that demotes them.
#
# **`allow_indirect_imports = true` on both, and the second one was measured
# rather than assumed.** With the flag off it goes red on today's tree naming
# three modules that have done nothing: every path it reports runs through
# `app.bootstrap.composition`, which imports every adapter on the platform by
# design, so a strict contract here is a contract about the composition root and
# not about the matcher. The violation this exists for is the **direct** one, and
# it is direct in the only shape it could realistically take: a composing adapter
# in `matchmaking/infrastructure/`, spelled exactly like `games_match_history.py`
# beside it, reading a star average into a term. That import names
# `app.modules.sportsmanship` on its own line.
#
# The engines need no arm here: `engines-are-pure` above already forbids them
# `app.modules` outright, and an arm that restates a rule is an arm that looks
# like it is doing work.
[importlinter:contract:sportsmanship-does-not-know-the-matcher]
name = The Sportsmanship context does not import the matcher
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.sportsmanship
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.matchmaking
allow_indirect_imports = true

[importlinter:contract:the-matcher-never-reads-sportsmanship]
name = The matcher never reads sportsmanship — not the floor, not a term
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.matchmaking
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.sportsmanship
allow_indirect_imports = true

# `scripts/` holds the offline machines — the ones that read a document, run the
# engine over it and write the answer back. `scripts/__init__.py` says nothing
# under `app/` may import from here, and this is what makes that true: the
# harness reaches into `docs/` and shells out at a shape no shipped code should
# depend on, and a use case importing it would put a repository path in a
# request. The direction it forbids is the only one that is wrong — `scripts`
# imports `app` on every line, which is the whole point of it.
[importlinter:contract:the-app-never-imports-its-offline-tooling]
name = No shipped code imports scripts/
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app
forbidden_modules =
    scripts
allow_indirect_imports = false

# The dev console is a development tool that lives inside the backend, and two
# arrows keep that from being a claim nobody checks.
#
# **The player surface never imports it.** The leak that matters is a dev route
# reaching the shipped client contract — `openapi.player.json` is what
# `frontend_player` generates its wire types from, and a type that arrived from
# a dev tool would ship in an app-store release. `tests/unit/test_dev_console_surface.py`
# asserts the document itself carries no `/dev` path; this forbids the import
# that would put one there.
#
# **The console does not import the seeds.** It is *entitled* to — both are
# gated by the same environments — and it declines, for the reason
# `app/api/dev/__init__.py` writes down: `app/seeds/__init__` refuses on import
# too, so a console that imported it would refuse in production for the seed
# table's reason and `CONSOLE_SERVED` would be a second table nothing ever
# proved fires.
#
# Both set `allow_indirect_imports = false`, and the same sentence earns it
# twice: the interesting violation is the indirect one. A helper module that
# reaches the seed catalog on the console's behalf breaks the seed rule exactly
# as completely, and a shared module that pulls a dev schema into the player
# surface ships it in `openapi.player.json` exactly as completely. Both are
# green on today's tree with the flag off, which is what makes it affordable —
# the argument the imaging fence already makes one contract up.
[importlinter:contract:the-player-surface-does-not-know-the-dev-console]
name = The player API surface does not import the dev console
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.api.v1
    app.modules
forbidden_modules =
    app.api.dev
allow_indirect_imports = false

[importlinter:contract:the-dev-console-declines-the-seed-import]
name = The dev console does not import the seed system it is entitled to
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.api.dev
forbidden_modules =
    app.seeds
allow_indirect_imports = false

# The player record is an **assembler**: it stores nothing, and every fact on the
# sheet it draws is somebody else's (docs/adr/0155). That makes it the one context
# on this platform whose domain and application layers have a standing reason to
# reach sideways — the type it wants usually exists already, one directory over,
# with the right shape and the wrong owner. `CardIdentity` is the example the ADR
# argues about: three of its fields are exactly this sheet's header, and reusing it
# would make a record inherit a *card's* closed list, which ADR-0107 keeps short
# because a card is attached over twenty rows and frozen into a record kept for
# ever. A record is one person, live, opened by a deliberate tap.
#
# So the rule is that this context declares its **own** ports and its own types,
# and the translation happens in `infrastructure/`, where every adapter here is a
# consumer-driven contract satisfied over somebody else's published port
# (ADR-0098). That is a rule about import statements, which is exactly the kind
# broken by somebody in a hurry, and nothing else would go red: an application
# layer importing `CardIdentity` would look like a sensible reuse in the diff.
#
# `allow_indirect_imports = false`, and it is affordable because these two layers
# genuinely import nothing but the kernel and each other. The strict flag is what
# catches the interesting version — a domain module that reaches somebody else's
# type through a shared helper has borrowed it exactly as completely.
#
# The forbidden list is **spelled out rather than wildcarded**, and that carries a
# cost worth naming: a context nobody has written yet is not on it, so the day an
# eleventh arrives its types may be borrowed here with the build green. A wildcard
# would not fix it — `app.modules` as a forbidden module also forbids this package
# from importing itself, which is the same trap the two engine contracts above are
# split in half to avoid. So the row is owed by whoever writes the eleventh
# context, and this paragraph is where they are told.
[importlinter:contract:the-player-record-declares-its-own-ports]
name = The player record's domain and application layers borrow no other context's types
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    app.modules.player_record.domain
    app.modules.player_record.application
forbidden_modules =
    app.modules.auth
    app.modules.blocks
    app.modules.challenges
    app.modules.games
    app.modules.matchmaking
    app.modules.player_profile
    app.modules.player_score
    app.modules.rating
    app.modules.sportsmanship
    app.modules.users
allow_indirect_imports = false
